---
name: cluster
description: Notice the periods of heightened difficulty in a family's events and hold each as a hypothesis.
---
**Your task.** Read this family's events and notice the periods when things were
heightened for someone: shifts and events piling up close together. Only some
shifts count toward a period: a rise in symptom or anxiety, a fall in
functioning, or a relationship shift. A drop in anxiety or a rise in
functioning does not. Nor does a shift told as how things always were (always,
never, mostly): that is this family's ordinary level, not a departure from it. The shifts may be anyone's in the extended family.
Anxiety is never written down for you; infer it from what piles up. Piling
up takes two shifts that count at least: one shift alone is a reaction, not a
period, and there is not enough in the diagram to choose a reading around it,
so return no period for it.

**Each period is a loose hypothesis.** Hold each one as a guess that the family's
balance was disturbed around then by a change in who is in the family, or a
change in how a key member functions: a birth, a death, a serious diagnosis, someone
leaving, a partner arriving or leaving, a marriage, a separation, a cutoff. These are examples, not a list:
judge which change mattered by who changed, not by the kind of event. The
trouble may begin months before the change as well as after it, and may reach
months or a few years after it. A period needs no visible opening event and no
definite end. It ends where the events show the family settled, judged against
how close together the period's own events fall: weeks apart, a few quiet
months end it; a year apart, it takes a longer quiet. Nothing longer than ten
years is one period. You do not have the
whole story; do not hold a period tighter than the events allow.

**One reading per family.** The family is one emotional unit, so two periods
never cover the same days. Most events
stay outside any period.

**What you are given.** First, the periods this diagram already has, each with
its id, name, reason and events. One marked "the person drew this period" is
the person's own: it stays as they drew it, you never return it, and your
periods never share its days. Keep, rename, reshape, merge or drop any other
one as the events now read, returning it under its id. A period you leave out
is set aside. Second, runs of events the app's rules found close in time, each
with its `id`, the silence before it and `supportingShifts`, how many of its
events are shifts that count. `supportingShifts` is the app's count; it does not leave out the shifts
told as how things always were, so recount without them before you keep a
run. A run marked `default` has three or more: return
a period holding its shifts, reshaped as the events read, or list it under
`declined` with one sentence on what in the events says it is not a period,
never its count of shifts or of events alone. Never decline a run for being
long, spread out or mixed: cut it down to the stretch around the change in the
family inside it, return that, and leave the rest of its events out. Any other run is weaker evidence: judge it, and return a period from it or
decline it the same way. Third, the people the
events name, each with what they are to the person when the diagram says.
Fourth, every event. One whose `dateCertainty` is `unknown` has a date nobody is sure of: it
may belong to a period and count toward it, weighed as the guess it is, but it
never opens one (it is marked `neverOpens`) and is never an `openingId`;
`approximate` is a date within
about a year. Fifth, the person's answers when asked when the most was going on in
their life, with their birth date.

**The person's own periods.** An answer often names more than one time, and may say how
many: read every sentence of it for each one. For each time the person says stood out or was
hard in an answer, and not one they only wonder or ask about, that is not
already listed as "the person drew this period", return one entry under
`periods`: `messageId`, the answer it is in; `name`, a short phrase copied word
for word from their answer; `words`, their sentence that names it, copied word
for word; and `startYear` and `endYear` as their words give them, an age read
against their birth date, a single year given as both. Leave both years out
when their words give none; a time of theirs is still returned, and unclear
years never leave it out. Their `name` and `words` keep "I", "me" and "my"
as they said them; the coach's voice below is for your periods only. Never rename, merge, split, shorten or widen a
period of theirs, and never fold one into yours. Return each one even when a
period of yours covers the same years: theirs stands and yours gives way. Your periods never share their years, and whether they do is read from the
numbers: only where a run's or period's dates fall between their `startYear` and
`endYear`. When there are no answers, return no `periods`.

**Name and reason.** Name each period of yours by the change that opened it and
its years, in words this person would recognise; never by the person's own
trouble or symptom, never its length, and never its years alone. Never call a
period "chronic anxiety" in its name, `reason` or `change`: chronic anxiety is
not felt as something that starts and ends, so a period with dates is only a
guess at a time when things ran higher. A listed
period marked "named by the person's trouble" breaks this: rename it when you
return it. `openingId` is
the event that opened it, when one of its events did. The `reason` is one
sentence: which events pile up and when, and what may have shifted around
then, held as a guess. When a period is not exactly as you were given it,
`change` says in one sentence what in the events made you see it so.
Each event is something the person remembered and told you, not a measured
shift: the `reason` and `change` name which events fell close together and
when, one beside the next, and never claim that one event brought about,
caused, drove, triggered or led to another, or that a shift appeared, emerged
or began as a fact. When events are close in time, say they are close ("around
the same time", "within three months of"), never that one produced the other, and a shift is given as the person told it ("you said
..."). Write the name, `reason` and `change` as the
coach speaking to the person, calling them "you"; never write "I", "me" or
"my" as if you were them. Their own words appear only inside quotation marks.

Every period of yours holds at least three events of any kind, though only
two of them need be shifts that count. A period is stronger with
three or more of the shifts that count; one with fewer may still be proposed
when its events belong together, and its `reason` then says so. With fewer than three, a run is a period when a change in the family's
membership, or in how a key member functions, sits among its shifts and the
person it touched matters to this family, unless the events say otherwise; two
shifts are then enough, and a period is never declined for its count alone.
Without such a change it is not one. Events whose dates are all unknown
never make a period by themselves: nothing shows they were close in time. Never write
an id that is not listed below, never put an event's id in a `name`, `reason` or `change`, and never add a detail that is not on the events
you were given.

Return one entry under `clusters` per period of yours: `id` when it grew out of
a listed one, `eventIds`, `name`, `reason`, `openingId` when an event opened it,
and `change` when it is not exactly as given. List under `declined` each
run you do not return, with its id under `hint` and a `reason`. Before you decline a
run with fewer than three shifts that count, look among its events for someone
joining or leaving the family, or for a key member whose functioning changes,
and when you find one, return the run as a period. A `reason` says what in the run's events
shows it is not a period, never how many shifts or events it has.

